## Install dependencies

Install the project's Python dependencies and create its `uv.lock`:

```bash
uv sync
```

## Set up renderers

`views/` is the project's renderer package (`[tool.fxtr.views]`). Register renderers in
`views/src/bundle.ts` to customize how the viewer shows your data. {{#published}}With Node 22
or later and pnpm 10 installed,{{/published}}{{^published}}Follow the JavaScript setup in the fxtr
checkout's `README.md` to select Node, then{{/published}} install dependencies with
`pnpm install` in `views/`. `uv run fxtr view` builds the renderers when it starts and rebuilds
them whenever you change them, and the open viewer shows each build without a reload. To build
them without the viewer:

```bash
uv run fxtr views build
```

`views/pnpm-workspace.yaml` gives this package its own installation and lockfile{{#in_checkout}},
even inside the fxtr checkout{{/in_checkout}}. Commit `views/pnpm-lock.yaml` along with the project
before launching jobs.
{{#vendored}}

`views/vendor/fxtr/` is a copy of the fxtr TypeScript packages the renderers use, made from the
fxtr this project was created with; commit it with the project. After upgrading fxtr, refresh it
and reinstall:

```bash
uv run fxtr views vendor
(cd views && pnpm install) && uv run fxtr views build
```
{{/vendored}}

## Configure the database

{{#published}}
Jobs run on a Postgres database. To run one locally with Docker:

```bash
docker run -d --name fxtr-postgres -p 55433:5432 \
  -e POSTGRES_USER=fxtr -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=fxtr -e POSTGRES_DB=fxtr postgres:16
```

Then say where this machine reaches it:
{{/published}}
{{^published}}
Say where this machine reaches the project's database. The server that `postgres_env/` in the
fxtr checkout runs is reached this way:
{{/published}}

```bash
uv run fxtr init --database-url postgresql+asyncpg://fxtr:fxtr@localhost:55433/fxtr
```

## Add API keys

Put the API keys of the model providers your experiments call, such as `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` and
`OPENAI_API_KEY`, in `.env`. Every launch, `fxtr run`, and `fxtr resume` reads it into the
environment, overriding variables set in your shell (an empty value sets nothing). `.env` is
gitignored: never commit it, and never put keys in experiment inputs or entities, which the
database stores.
{{^example}}

## Write your first experiment

This project starts without experiment code or a launcher. Define your steps and workflows in
`src/{{package}}/`, add their module names to `[tool.fxtr] modules` in `pyproject.toml`, and
write a launcher at the project root. Commit your code before launching: every job records
the commit it runs, and checks that the venv matches `uv.lock`.

[Your first experiment](https://docs.transluce.ai/fxtr/first-experiment) walks you through
designing and running an experiment of your own in this project, with your coding agent as a
guide.
{{/example}}
{{#example}}

## Run the example

This project includes a complete greeting experiment in `src/{{package}}/experiment.py`.
Its `launch.py` runs it as a Postgres job and prints greetings for Ada, Alan, and Grace.

```bash
git add . && git commit -m "A new fxtr project"
uv run python launch.py
uv run fxtr run {{workflow}} --input salutation=Hello   # the same, by the workflow's name
uv run fxtr jobs list
```

A launch pins the code it runs, so it refuses uncommitted changes, and checks that the venv matches
`uv.lock` (`uv sync`). The `fxtr run` command accepts `--allow-staged` to pin staged changes.

Steps are cached by where they are in the job (`root/greet[guest:"Ada"]`), across jobs: the second
launch above reuses the first's greetings. A job asking one of those keys for something else (another
salutation or registered step name) stops on a *cache conflict* at the key; its status says how to
list, clear, or overwrite the conflicts. To rerun such a launch and replace the old results:

```bash
uv run fxtr run {{workflow}} --input salutation=Hi --overwrite-cache-conflicts
```

Changing only a step's implementation does not invalidate its cached result; clear the affected
entries or choose a new cache namespace to rerun it:

```bash
uv run fxtr cache clear root/greet
```

`uv run fxtr --help` lists the rest (`resume`, `jobs`, `cache`).
{{/example}}

## Watching jobs

{{#published}}
`uv run fxtr view` serves the experiment viewer with this project's jobs and opens it in a
browser. Keep it running: each launch shows its job in the viewer's open tabs, or in a new tab
when none is open.
{{/published}}
{{^published}}
Build the viewer once in the fxtr checkout (`cd {{fxtr_checkout}} && pnpm install && pnpm --filter
fxtr-ide build`), then `uv run fxtr view` serves it with this project's jobs and opens it in a
browser. Keep it running: each launch shows its job in the viewer's open tabs, or in a new tab
when none is open.
{{/published}}

## Layout

```
pyproject.toml        the project, and [tool.fxtr]: the modules that register its steps and workflows
.env                  API keys, read into the environment of each launch; gitignored
{{#example}}
launch.py             calls launch_cli with its defaults to run a Postgres job
{{/example}}
src/{{package}}/
    __init__.py       the Python package
{{#example}}
    experiment.py     the entity type, the step, and the workflow
{{/example}}
views/                the renderer package: src/bundle.ts is what it contributes to the viewer
{{#vendored}}
    vendor/fxtr/      the fxtr TypeScript packages it links, copied by `fxtr views vendor`
{{/vendored}}
```
